/*
Idea:
Let dp[i] be whether the player who wants x=0 can force a win from round i onward
for a given current xor value x.

A cleaner way:
For suffix starting at i, define W_i as the set of xor values x such that
the player 0 (who wants final x=0) wins from state (i, x) under optimal play.

Terminal:
After all rounds, W_N = {0}.

Transition for round i with controller s = S[i], value a = A[i]:
From current x, next state is either x or x^a.

- If s == 0 (player 0 chooses), then x is winning iff at least one of
  x in W_{i+1} or x^a in W_{i+1}.
  So W_i = W_{i+1} union (W_{i+1} xor a)

- If s == 1 (player 1 chooses), then x is winning for player 0 iff both choices
  still lead to winning states for player 0.
  So W_i = W_{i+1} intersection (W_{i+1} xor a)

A key observation:
Starting from {0}, repeated operations of union/intersection with its xor-shift
always produce a linear subspace over xor.
For a subspace V:
- V union (V xor a) is:
    V, if a in V
    span(V, a), otherwise
- V intersection (V xor a) is:
    V, if a in V
    empty, otherwise

Thus we only need to track:
- whether the winning set is empty
- otherwise, its xor-basis

Process rounds backwards:
Start with subspace {0}.
For each round:
- if player 0:
    if a not in span, insert it
- if player 1:
    if a not in span, winning set becomes empty forever

At the end, initial x=0 is winning iff winning set is non-empty
(because 0 belongs to every non-empty xor-subspace), otherwise losing.

So answer "0" if player 0 can force final x=0, else "1".

Complexity: O(total N * 60)
*/

public class Main {
    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do {
                c = read();
            } while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }

        String next() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do {
                c = read();
            } while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            while (c > ' ') {
                sb.append((char) c);
                c = read();
            }
            return sb.toString();
        }
    }

    static class XorBasis {
        long[] basis = new long[61]; // enough for values up to around 1e18

        boolean contains(long x) {
            long v = x;
            for (int b = 60; b >= 0; b--) {
                if (((v >> b) & 1L) == 0) continue;
                if (basis[b] == 0) return false;
                v ^= basis[b];
            }
            return true;
        }

        void add(long x) {
            long v = x;
            for (int b = 60; b >= 0; b--) {
                if (((v >> b) & 1L) == 0) continue;
                if (basis[b] == 0) {
                    basis[b] = v;
                    return;
                }
                v ^= basis[b];
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();

        int T = fs.nextInt();
        while (T-- > 0) {
            int N = fs.nextInt();
            long[] A = new long[N];
            for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) A[i] = fs.nextLong();

            String s = fs.next();
            // If input gives S as spaced integers instead of a string, this problem's format
            // would differ, but standard format is a binary string.

            XorBasis basis = new XorBasis();
            boolean empty = false; // false means current winning set is a non-empty subspace

            for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                if (empty) break;
                char c = s.charAt(i);
                long a = A[i];
                boolean in = basis.contains(a);

                if (c == '0') {
                    if (!in) basis.add(a);
                } else {
                    if (!in) empty = true;
                }
            }

            out.append(empty ? '1' : '0').append('\n');
        }

        System.out.print(out.toString());
    }
}

